/// What a run of source bytes is.
///
/// Enough kinds to **tile**: every byte of a file belongs to exactly one
/// token, trivia included, so re-joining the runs reproduces the file exactly.
/// That is this lexer's whole contract, and it is the one a formatter needs.
///
/// The Rust formatter does not have it. `cove_syntax::ast` carries `///` doc
/// comments and drops `//`, `/* */` and the blank lines an author wrote, so
/// `format_source` reads them back out of the source by position and
/// re-attaches them — and its own documentation says a comment it cannot place
/// exactly is moved to the nearest following boundary. A lossless token stream
/// removes that whole class of difficulty rather than solving it again.
export enum Kind {
  /// Spaces, tabs, carriage returns and newlines. A blank line an author
  /// wrote is a run of these, which is how it survives at all.
  Space
  /// `// ...` to the end of the line, the newline not included.
  LineComment
  /// `/* ... */`, which nests.
  BlockComment
  /// `/// ...`, which the parser attaches to what follows it.
  DocComment
  /// A `"..."` literal, interpolations and all. What is inside `{ }` is one
  /// more expression and is the parser's to take apart; as a *run of source*
  /// it belongs to the literal.
  Str
  /// A `'a'` code-point literal, which is an `Int` — ADR 0047.
  Char
  /// A number, its fraction and its duration suffix: `500ms` is one token.
  Number
  /// An identifier or a keyword. Which of the two is the parser's question:
  /// a lexer that answered it would need the keyword list, and nothing here
  /// is decided by it.
  Word
  /// One operator or one bracket, longest match first: `..<` is one token
  /// and not three.
  Punct
  /// A byte no rule above claims. It is a token like any other so that the
  /// tiling holds for a file that does not lex, which is the normal case
  /// while somebody is typing.
  Unknown
}

/// One run of source, named by its kind and its half-open byte range.
///
/// A byte range and not a character range: `String` hands out byte offsets
/// (ADR 0046) and `sliceBytes` cuts the run back out, so a token costs three
/// words and no copy of the text it names.
export struct Token {
  kind: Kind
  from: Int
  to: Int
}
